1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Good morning and welcome to Kennedy Space Center in Florida where we will soon launch NASA's Nancy Grace-Roman Space Telescope.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
This journey started more than 20 years ago when we just had Roman as pencil and paper sketches

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
and now it stands waiting on the launch pad ready to go to space and reveal the secrets of the universe.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
I'm beyond excited for the NASA Goddard Space Flight team, the NASA JPL team,

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:31,000
literally the thousands that have come together to deliver NASA's great exploration asset right now

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,000
under budget and actually ahead of schedule.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Roman was designed to address key questions that people have asked for a very long time.

8
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:46,000
One of them is about exoplanets and whether there are planets out there and solar systems out there like our own.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
The other is about the biggest questions on the grandest scales.

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:56,000
The fabric of the universe, how the universe that we see around us came to be pushed and pulled by the competing forces

11
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,000
of dark matter and dark energy.

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,000
These are mysteries that Roman was designed to settle.

13
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Three, two, one, ignition and lift off.

14
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Go Falcon Heavy and go Roman Space Telescope.

15
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,830
We're going to open Roman's eyes.

16
00:01:16,830 --> 00:01:22,830
We're going to see the sky in a new way with Roman for the first time and we're just going to get blown away.

17
00:01:23,830 --> 00:01:28,830
When we send Roman out there a million miles away, it will take about three months to get out to that point

18
00:01:28,830 --> 00:01:33,830
during which time we are turning on the instruments, aligning the telescope, calibrating everything

19
00:01:33,830 --> 00:01:37,830
so that we are ready to begin efficient observations as soon as we're there.

20
00:01:37,830 --> 00:01:41,830
What new things will we discover that we don't even conceive up at the moment?

21
00:01:41,830 --> 00:01:45,830
What questions will Roman answer that we don't even know how to formulate yet?

22
00:01:45,830 --> 00:01:50,830
That's what's going to make this mission transformative and a civilization scale experiment.

23
00:01:50,830 --> 00:01:59,830
We built this in order to provide emotionally impactful and scientifically impactful imagery for all of humanity

24
00:01:59,830 --> 00:02:02,830
and I feel like we have managed to achieve that today.

